    <!-- SECTION 1 -->
    <div class="page-break" id="sec1">
        <h2>1. Executive Summary &amp; High-Level Timeline</h2>
        <p>
            The <strong>Afrobotics Brand Insertion Technology (BIT)</strong> platform is an AI-powered in-content advertising solution. It transforms suitable surfaces inside produced video content (such as billboards, television screens, stadium signage, and product labels) into monetizable, dynamic ad placements. This document outlines the accelerated, professional 14-day delivery roadmap to design, build, test, and release the platform.
        </p>
        <p>
            To meet the aggressive time-to-market constraints, the project is structured into two strict phases:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Phase 1: Frontend UI &amp; Backend API (Days 1 to 5)</strong> - Focuses on establishing the relational database model, creating the secure web management console in Vue.js, and implementing the complete RESTful C# ASP.NET Core API layer. At the end of Day 5, all UI workflows (approvals, campaign management, video staging) are fully interactive and integrated with mock service endpoints.</li>
            <li><strong>Phase 2: AI Engine &amp; Pipeline Integration (Days 6 to 14)</strong> - Focuses on establishing the object-oriented C# computer vision interfaces, wrapping advanced CV models (YOLO, Segment Anything Model 2, OpenCV trackers), integrating with high-performance GPU rendering nodes, enforcing mandatory brand-safety filters, and carrying out end-to-end load, recovery, and quality assurance testing.</li>
        </ul>
        
        <div class="highlight-box">
            <strong>Key Project Constraint:</strong> The full system must be delivered in exactly 2 weeks. The UI and API must be completed and fully functional (using mock services where needed) within the first 5 days. The remainder of the timeline (9 days) is dedicated exclusively to the computer vision engine integration, compositing/rendering pipelines, and comprehensive platform testing.
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 2 -->
    <div class="page-break" id="sec2">
        <h2>2. Decoupled Architecture &amp; Flexible AI Engine Design</h2>
        <p>
            A core business and technical requirement of Afrobotics BIT is that <strong>the AI engine must not be rigid</strong>. As computer-vision research advances, the platform must allow different models (e.g., swapping SAM 2 for a newer segmentation model, or changing YOLO to custom object detection) to be evaluated and swapped with ease. 
        </p>
        <p>
            To satisfy this requirement without introducing downstream UI breakage or requiring heavy code rewrites, the architecture utilizes a strict <strong>decoupled design pattern</strong> driven by Object-Oriented C# interfaces:
        </p>
        
        <h3>2.1 The Agnostic Frontend (Vue.js)</h3>
        <p>
            The Vue.js user interface has <strong>zero awareness</strong> of the underlying machine learning models or GPU compositing engines. The UI communicates with the backend solely through standardized, strongly-typed RESTful API contracts (JSON payloads). For example, when displaying detected advertising surfaces on a video frame, the UI receives a list of standardized geometric coordinates, confidence scores, and bounding boxes. Whether those coordinates were calculated by SAM 2, YOLO, or manually drawn, the Vue.js rendering canvas processes them identically.
        </p>

        <h3>2.2 Standard C# Service Interfaces</h3>
        <p>
            In the C# backend, all core AI capabilities are modeled as abstraction layers using interfaces. The rest of the application (e.g., the campaign coordinators, the workflow engines, and the API endpoints) depends strictly on these interfaces rather than concrete classes.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>ISurfaceDetectionEngine:</strong> Defines contracts for taking a video scene index and returning list of candidate bounding shapes, depth indicators, and orientation statistics.</li>
            <li><strong>IMotionTracker:</strong> Defines contracts for tracking standard coordinate frames across subsequent frames, managing camera pan/zoom offsets, and returning stabilization confidence vectors.</li>
            <li><strong>ICompositingEngine:</strong> Defines contracts for performing homography warping, color/temperature light matching, noise matching, and shadow projection.</li>
            <li><strong>IBrandSafetyEvaluator:</strong> Defines contracts for checking detected surfaces against permanent and configurable exclusion lists (e.g., face detection, religious symbols) before scoring.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>2.3 Seamless Hot-Swapping via Dependency Injection</h3>
        <p>
            Multiple concrete classes can be created for each interface. For instance, `Sam2SurfaceDetector` and `YoloSurfaceDetector` both implement the `ISurfaceDetectionEngine` interface. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Swapping the active AI engine is a single-line configuration change in the C# Dependency Injection container (located in `Program.cs` or `Startup.cs`). The C# runtime automatically resolves and injects the selected engine throughout the entire processing pipeline. No UI code is ever modified, no databases are re-structured, and no API controllers are rewritten. This enables the engineering team to run active A/B tests and swap model combinations effortlessly until the optimal visual and performance threshold is achieved.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 3 -->
    <div class="page-break" id="sec3">
        <h2>3. Core Database Relational Model (PostgreSQL)</h2>
        <p>
            To support high transaction volumes, campaign state machines, and detailed audit trails, a robust, relational PostgreSQL schema is implemented. Large binary files (source videos, brand assets, finished renders) are stored in cloud-hosted Object Storage (e.g., AWS S3 or Azure Blob) and referenced in the database via secure URLs and unique storage keys.
        </p>
        
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th style="width: 20%;">Entity Name</th>
                    <th style="width: 30%;">Purpose</th>
                    <th style="width: 50%;">Mandatory Fields &amp; Data Types</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Content</strong></td>
                    <td>Represents ingested source videos.</td>
                    <td>ContentID (UUID), Title (Varchar), Duration (Interval), Resolution (Varchar), FrameRate (Numeric), SourceChannel (Varchar), StorageKey (Varchar), IngestionStatus (Enum), AuditLog (JSONB), CreatedAt (Timestamp), ModifiedAt (Timestamp).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Scene</strong></td>
                    <td>Indexed cuts and logical segments of a video.</td>
                    <td>SceneID (UUID), ContentID (FK), StartFrame (Integer), EndFrame (Integer), SceneIndex (Integer), DurationSeconds (Numeric), QA_Status (Enum), CreatedAt (Timestamp).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Surface</strong></td>
                    <td>Candidate ad placements detected within a scene.</td>
                    <td>SurfaceID (UUID), SceneID (FK), SurfaceType (Varchar), BoundaryCoordinates (JSONB), EstimatedDepth (Numeric), OrientationVector (JSONB), ConfidenceScore (Numeric), ViabilityScore (Numeric), Status (Varchar), CreatedAt (Timestamp).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>AdSlot</strong></td>
                    <td>Approved, monetizable placement inventory.</td>
                    <td>AdSlotID (UUID), SurfaceID (FK), MarketRegion (Varchar), PricingValue (Decimal), SlotStatus (Enum), Dimensions (Varchar), CampaignID (FK, Nullable), CreatedAt (Timestamp).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Campaign</strong></td>
                    <td>Advertiser campaign parameters and schedule.</td>
                    <td>CampaignID (UUID), AdvertiserID (FK), CampaignName (Varchar), NamingStructureCode (Varchar), ScheduleStart (Timestamp), ScheduleEnd (Timestamp), TargetRegion (Varchar), TotalBudget (Decimal), Status (Enum), CreatedAt (Timestamp).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>CreativeAsset</strong></td>
                    <td>The brand visual (logo/video/image) to be placed.</td>
                    <td>AssetID (UUID), AdvertiserID (FK), AssetType (Varchar), StorageKey (Varchar), FileSize (BigInt), OriginalDimensions (Varchar), BrandCategory (Varchar), CreatedAt (Timestamp).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Render</strong></td>
                    <td>Final composited and baked video exports.</td>
                    <td>RenderID (UUID), ContentID (FK), AdSlotID (FK), CampaignID (FK), ExportPreset (Varchar), StorageKey (Varchar), RenderStatus (Enum), ProcessingDurationMs (Integer), CreatedAt (Timestamp).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>User &amp; Role</strong></td>
                    <td>Accounts and role-based permissions (RBAC).</td>
                    <td>UserID (UUID), FullName (Varchar), Email (Varchar, Unique), PasswordHash (Varchar), Roles (Array of Enums), AccountStatus (Enum), LastLoginAt (Timestamp), CreatedAt (Timestamp).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Approval</strong></td>
                    <td>Audit logs for human-in-the-loop validation.</td>
                    <td>ApprovalID (UUID), AdSlotID (FK), CampaignID (FK), ApproverUserID (FK), Decision (Enum), RejectionReason (Text), Timestamp (Timestamp).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>BrandSafetyRule</strong></td>
                    <td>Permanent exclusion list constraints.</td>
                    <td>RuleID (UUID), CategoryName (Varchar), Description (Text), ExcludedObjects (Array), IsActive (Boolean, Permanent True), CreatedAt (Timestamp).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Notification</strong></td>
                    <td>Logs of SMS and Email notifications.</td>
                    <td>NotificationID (UUID), Recipient (Varchar), MediumType (Enum), Subject (Varchar), BodyText (Text), DispatchStatus (Enum), ErrorCode (Varchar), CreatedAt (Timestamp).</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 4 -->
    <div class="page-break" id="sec4">
        <h2>4. Phase 1: UI &amp; REST API Layer (Days 1–5)</h2>
        <p>
            The objective of Phase 1 is to deliver a fully functional, highly interactive user experience and API schema. By the end of Day 5, a business user can log in, navigate all panels, create mock campaigns, upload assets, and review simulated video detections.
        </p>
        
        <h3>Day 1: PostgreSQL Setup, Data Models, &amp; Authentication (MReq 8, 9, 25)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Provision the PostgreSQL database instance and execute DDL migrations to build all tables, indexes, and referential constraints as detailed in Section 3.</li>
            <li>Implement the C# identity service using JWT (JSON Web Tokens) over secure HTTPS, enabling granular Role-Based Access Control (RBAC).</li>
            <li>Seed initial default administrative and editor roles to allow access to the testing panels.</li>
            <li>Ensure rigid server-side payload validation is active to intercept corrupted requests before hitting data tables.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Day 2: Vue.js UI Foundation &amp; C# API Scaffolding (MReq 8, 10, 18)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Scaffold the Vue.js single-page application using Tailwind CSS, implementing a modern, high-contrast theme focused on large-scale video metrics.</li>
            <li>Establish global state management for the user profile, active session counters, and interface status.</li>
            <li>Scaffold all C# ASP.NET Core REST controllers (`CampaignController`, `InventoryController`, `UserController`) and register basic API routing with appropriate authorization attributes.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Day 3: Campaign &amp; Asset Library Dashboards (MReq 10, 13)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Develop the Vue.js Advertiser and Agency dashboards. Build forms for campaign creation, including strict naming rules (e.g., `UZ01EP12_COKE`) to prevent operational errors.</li>
            <li>Create the Asset Library UI supporting drag-and-drop file uploads for creative assets.</li>
            <li>Implement backend C# upload controllers that interface securely with S3 or Azure Blob storage to stage original assets, returning storage metadata.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Day 4: Content Ingestion Interface &amp; Scene QA Portal (MReq 1, 13)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Build the Content Ingestion UI for video administrators. This allows uploading large source files (MP4, MOV, MXF, AVI).</li>
            <li>Implement C# API endpoints using backend FFmpeg wrappers to read video durations, resolutions, frame rates, and extract metadata.</li>
            <li>Build validation rules that forbid altering content duration or warping core footage, ensuring the complete preservation of original video files.</li>
            <li>Create a Scene cut-detection preview screen that allows administrators to run Scene Quality Assurance (QA).</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Day 5: Placement Approval, Notifications, &amp; Admin Panel (MReq 11, 12, 15, 18)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Design and build the Editor/Approver visual workbench. Editors can review list of candidate placements on a frame-by-frame player, with buttons to Approve or Reject. Rejections require input of a structured reason.</li>
            <li>Implement C# endpoints for recording approvals, immediately creating a permanent audit trail.</li>
            <li>Integrate SMTP and SMS dispatch queues, sending immediate notifications when render workloads are complete or campaigns are assigned.</li>
            <li>Complete the web-based Administration interface, providing central control over active users, background task statuses, and server telemetry.</li>
        </ul>
        
        <div class="highlight-box" style="background-color: #F0FDF4; border-left-color: #16A34A;">
            <strong>Day 5 Delivery Milestone:</strong> The complete Vue.js frontend and C# REST API are fully functional, styled, and compiled. Behind-the-scenes pipeline requests return perfectly structured simulated data. The client and business sponsors can interact with the app in the browser and sign-off on the usability model.
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 5 -->
    <div class="page-break" id="sec5">
        <h2>5. Phase 2: AI Engine Integration &amp; Testing (Days 6–14)</h2>
        <p>
            Phase 2 transforms the simulated interface into an intelligent, highly automated video inventory and compositing pipeline. This phase resolves technical complexities regarding tracking, perspective, and brand safety.
        </p>
        
        <h3>Day 6: C# AI Abstraction Contracts &amp; Scene Cut Pipeline (MReq 1, 25)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Code the formal C# interface boundaries (`ISurfaceDetectionEngine`, `IMotionTracker`, `ICompositingEngine`, `IBrandSafetyEvaluator`) to establish decoupled pathways.</li>
            <li>Integrate the physical scene-cut detection engine. The backend processes the source video, registers scene markers, and populates the `Scene` PostgreSQL table with start/end frames.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Day 7: Computer-Vision Scene Analysis &amp; Brand Detection (MReq 2, 3)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Integrate SAM 2 (Segment Anything Model) and YOLO pipelines into concrete C# implementation services. These services detect walls, screens, billboards, and other candidate surfaces.</li>
            <li>Implement depth estimation and coordinate alignment to map 3D surface parameters.</li>
            <li>Develop the **Competitive Separation Algorithm**: scan frames for pre-existing commercial logos, text, or brand indicators in order to calculate surface viability and prevent placing a product near its competitor.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Day 8: Permanent Brand-Safety Exclusion Engine (MReq 4)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Implement the rigid, non-negotiable brand-safety enforcement layer within the core C# pipeline.</li>
            <li>Build mandatory object-detection filters that permanently block ads from being superimposed over human faces, children's clothes or products, emergency vehicles, government insignia, and religious symbols.</li>
            <li>Configure the system so that these core rules are hardcoded and cannot be bypassed or disabled by any user role (including administrators). Only additions are supported.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Day 9: Planar Motion Tracking &amp; Re-validation Engine (MReq 5)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Implement high-precision planar and point motion tracking (OpenCV based wrappers) to lock candidate surfaces to camera pans, zooms, and fast rotations.</li>
            <li>Develop fallback interpolation routines to lock surfaces during temporary occlusions or difficult cuts.</li>
            <li>Ensure the C# engine computes frame-to-frame drift parameters and flags any placement that exceeds a 2% slip-rate for manual Editor QA.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Day 10: Advanced Compositing &amp; VFX Simulation (MReq 6)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Build homography matrices in the compositing engine to warp 2D brand images and video assets into correct perspective matching the target surface.</li>
            <li>Implement shaders and light-matching filters that analyze surrounding pixel luminance to match light temperature, contrast, and brightness.</li>
            <li>Apply synthetic noise, soft drop-shadows, and motion blur parameters so the inserted ad matches original video characteristics perfectly.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Day 11: GPU Rendering Queue &amp; Multi-Format Exports (MReq 7, 14, 23)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Develop high-throughput dispatchers connecting the C# backend to virtualized GPU render instances (such as RunPod, EC2 GPU, or local hardware).</li>
            <li>Configure batch rendering schedules to execute multiple compositing workloads concurrently.</li>
            <li>Set up FFmpeg-based transcode parameters for high-fidelity exports: Broadcast-Ready (MXF/ProRes), Web/Streaming (MP4 H.264/H.265), and Social (Vertical/Mobile layouts).</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Day 12: Business Intelligence Dashboard &amp; System Health (MReq 16, 19, 20, 21, 22)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Link the database activity metrics to the Vue.js BI Dashboard. Display metrics such as: volume processed, impressions, estimated brand-exposure duration (seconds per spot), and average render times.</li>
            <li>Deploy the centralized Logging, Alarm, and Event framework. System generates high-severity alerts if the S3 bucket is disconnected, the GPU cluster returns an out-of-memory error, or the SQL queue stalls.</li>
            <li>Write automated usage records to single CSV files on scheduled intervals and archive them to a secure storage server.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Day 13: Redundancy &amp; Node Recovery Validation (MReq 17, 24)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Configure the system for multi-node load balancing. Test database locks and active task queues to ensure single-node crashes do not halt active background analysis.</li>
            <li>Simulate a hard failure of a rendering node mid-way through a video composite. Verify that the task manager detects the timeout, recovers the active state, and safely re-queues the render task on a backup GPU node without losing metadata.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Day 14: Final Acceptance Testing, Performance Tuning &amp; Launch (MReq 23, 24)</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Activities:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Perform formal human-in-the-loop QA across 100+ standard test clips, analyzing perspective accuracy, light adaptation, drift errors, and brand-safety compliance.</li>
            <li>Conduct a load and sizing audit. Ensure the platform processes an average of [xxx] minutes of content per node daily, keeping unit render costs well below the value of the generated ad slots.</li>
            <li>Hand over operational documentation, configuration files, and sign off for platform release.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 6 -->
    <div class="page-break" id="sec6">
        <h2>6. Mandatory Requirements (MReq) Mapping Matrix</h2>
        <p>
            To guarantee complete alignment and compliance with the project specifications, the table below maps each of the 25 Mandatory Requirements (MReqs) to its designated implementation day and delivery module.
        </p>
        
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th style="width: 15%;">Req ID</th>
                    <th style="width: 30%;">Mandatory Requirement Name</th>
                    <th style="width: 25%;">Implementation Phase</th>
                    <th style="width: 30%;">Target Delivery Window</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>MReq 1</strong></td>
