    <!-- SECTION 7 -->
    <div class="page-break" id="sec7">
        <h2>7. System Redundancy, Operations, &amp; Failure Modes</h2>
        <p>
            The Afrobotics BIT platform runs as a mission-critical advertising pipeline. Operating in live broadcasting environments dictates strict, non-negotiable standards for infrastructure resilience, telemetry monitoring, and audit compliance.
        </p>

        <h3>7.1 Infrastructure Redundancy (MReq 17)</h3>
        <p>
            To eliminate any single points of failure, the core software is deployed in a redundant cloud configuration (containerized nodes fronted by a high-availability Load Balancer).
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>State Preservation:</strong> All API requests are stateless. User sessions, analysis configurations, and render tasks are persisted in the PostgreSQL cluster, enabling seamless failover across web servers without disrupting user actions.</li>
            <li><strong>Robust Job Recovery:</strong> If a rendering or analysis node encounters a hard hardware failure mid-task, the system relies on a reliable distributed queuing model. The master process monitors active job heartbeats; if a node goes silent for more than 45 seconds, the task is flagged, recovered, and re-allocated to a healthy node in the GPU pool. No user file is lost, and no render requires manual re-submission.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>7.2 Monitoring, Alarms, &amp; Logging (MReq 20, 21, 22)</h3>
        <p>
            Operations, Administration, and Maintenance (OAM) rely on three core pillars:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Events Engine:</strong> Every functional change, background failure, or integration event generates a standardized log entry containing: Timestamp, Event Code, Severity, Source Module, Originating User, and Description.</li>
            <li><strong>Alarms Engine:</strong> Events are processed in real-time. If critical interfaces (such as Object Storage, SMTP Gateway, or the GPU render cluster) become unreachable, the platform raises a <em>Critical Alarm</em>. The alarm is immediately visualized on the Admin Console and dispatched via SMS/Email to on-call engineers. Once the connection is successfully re-established, the platform auto-generates a <em>Clear Alarm</em>, closing the incident loop.</li>
            <li><strong>Usage Record Auditing:</strong> A daily cron-job collates all usage logs (successful and failed API operations) into secure, tamper-proof CSV files. These files are compressed and transferred via secure shell protocols to an external archiving server for long-term audit compliance.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 8 -->
    <div class="page-break" id="sec8">
        <h2>8. Delivery Risk Mitigation &amp; Critical Milestones</h2>
        <p>
            Managing an accelerated 14-day timeline requires strict governance and clear boundaries between frontend development and complex backend computer-vision work. Below are the key mitigation protocols established to guarantee a risk-free delivery:
        </p>
        
        <div class="alert-box">
            <strong>Risk 1: AI Model Instability or Quality Drift (Mitigation Protocol)</strong><br>
            By establishing the C# Service Abstraction interfaces on Day 6, the team isolates tracking and compositing algorithms from the core REST API controllers. If a newly wrapped SAM 2 build performs poorly or encounters rendering drift, developers can immediately toggle back to YOLO/Detectron2 with a single configuration parameter in ASP.NET Core Dependency Injection. This decouples functional risk from the release timeline.
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background-color: #FEF3C7; border-left-color: #D97706;">
            <strong>Risk 2: GPU Render Compute Bottlenecks (Mitigation Protocol)</strong><br>
            To avoid slow render speeds, rendering queues are built as asynchronous tasks. The C# backend pushes render requests to a distributed pipeline, returning a progress ID instantly. The user interface does not block during renders; instead, it polls progress and displays a percentage. If GPU nodes fail, the job simply queues safely until healthy resource is restored.
        </div>

        <h3>8.1 Mandatory Quality Gate &amp; Sign-off Checkpoints</h3>
        <p>
            To guarantee project success, three formal gates are established across the 14-day window:
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Checkpoint 1 (Day 5 - UI/API Gate):</strong> Handover of the fully compiled Vue.js frontend and C# API controller layer. All web panels, tables, and forms must load in under 2 seconds and present realistic, fully interactive simulated data.</li>
            <li><strong>Checkpoint 2 (Day 11 - Pipeline Gate):</strong> Verification of the end-to-end video processing pipeline. Video files must successfully ingest, generate scene indexes, detect surfaces, enforce brand-safety rules, track movement, and output completed composited MP4 files via the GPU.</li>
            <li><strong>Checkpoint 3 (Day 14 - Final Acceptance):</strong> Formal validation of visual quality and system volume. Tracking drift must remain under 2% visual variance, perspective warping must look photorealistic, and throughput must sustain the targets set during sizing. Final sign-off secures official release deployment.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

